```
class Solution {
    // State graph: (location, hasSupplies). At a supply location you can switch 0 -> 1 for free.
    public int supplyRun(int n, int[][] routes, int landing, int base, int[] supplies) {
        List<List<int[]>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<int[]>());
        for (int[] r : routes) {
            adj.get(r[0]).add(new int[]{r[1], r[2]});
            adj.get(r[1]).add(new int[]{r[0], r[2]});
        }
        boolean[] isSupply = new boolean[n];
        for (int s : supplies) isSupply[s] = true;

        long[][] dist = new long[n][2];
        for (long[] row : dist) Arrays.fill(row, Long.MAX_VALUE);
        PriorityQueue<long[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(new Comparator<long[]>() {
            public int compare(long[] a, long[] b) { return Long.compare(a[0], b[0]); }
        });
        int s0 = isSupply[landing] ? 1 : 0;
        dist[landing][s0] = 0;
        pq.add(new long[]{0, landing, s0});
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            long[] top = pq.poll();
            long d = top[0];
            int u = (int) top[1], has = (int) top[2];
            if (d > dist[u][has]) continue;
            if (u == base && has == 1) return (int) d;
            for (int[] e : adj.get(u)) {
                int v = e[0];
                int nh = (has == 1 || isSupply[v]) ? 1 : 0;   // picking up supplies on arrival
                if (d + e[1] < dist[v][nh]) {
                    dist[v][nh] = d + e[1];
                    pq.add(new long[]{dist[v][nh], v, nh});
                }
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```

Two equivalent views for the "pick up then go to base" version:
1. Run Dijkstra from the landing site and from the base, then take **min over supply s of d1[s] + d2[s]**. Simple, but only works for exactly one intermediate stop.
2. The **state graph** (above). It generalizes to every row of the table, which is why it's the better thing to explain.

